.TH KISA-CCE-PATCH 8 "2026-09-04" "KISA CCE Linux Scanner" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
kisa-cce-patch \- plan, apply, verify, and roll back bounded KISA CCE
configuration and file-metadata remediation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kisa-cce-patch
.BI \-\-output-dir " NEW_PATH"
.RI [ \-\-root " PATH" ]
.RI [ \-\-checks " U-16,U-18" ]
.RI [ \-\-apply ]
.PP
.B kisa-cce-patch
.B \-\-automatic
.BI \-\-desired-state " FILE"
.RI [ \-\-root " PATH" ]
.RI [ \-\-output-dir " NEW_PATH" ]
.PP
.B kisa-cce-patch
.BI \-\-rollback " TRANSACTION_DIR"
.RI [ \-\-root " PATH" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kisa-cce-patch
is the dry-run-first configuration and metadata remediation companion to
.BR kisa-cce-scan (8).
Ordinary fixed-rule mode supports U-12, U-16, U-18, U-19, U-22, U-29, U-37,
U-62, and U-67.
U-12 and U-62 install fixed managed content. Five metadata rules use one fixed
logical path. U-37 and U-67 safely enumerate multiple targets within fixed path
scopes.
.PP
The command runs a fresh audit scan before planning and accepts only
.BR GOOD ,
.BR VULNERABLE ,
and
.B NOT_APPLICABLE
results.
Each vulnerable change candidate must also have
.BR resolution_class=technical ,
.BR remediation_eligible=true ,
and the exact versioned
.B remediation_rule_id
registered for its criterion.
The supported IDs are
.BR configuration.u12.v1 ,
.BR metadata.u16.v1 ,
.BR metadata.u18.v1 ,
.BR metadata.u19.v1 ,
.BR metadata.u22.v1 ,
.BR metadata.u29.v1 ,
.BR metadata.u37.v1 ,
.BR configuration.u62.v1 ,
and
.BR metadata.u67.v1 .
It completes root, path, configuration, and target metadata preflight for the
complete selection before making the first change.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-apply
or
.BR \-\-automatic ,
the command writes a protected plan and exits without modifying the assessed
root.
Apply mode installs only the fixed U-12 and U-62 content or changes an existing
metadata target's owner UID and permission bits. Metadata rules preserve GID.
Every apply runs a new scanner process for post-change verification. The command
does not accept arbitrary content or reload a service.
.PP
.B \-\-automatic
with
.B \-\-desired-state
is an explicit root-only request to assess and orchestrate U-01 through U-67.
It requires typed domain input, protected transactions, automatic rollback, and
a complete post-scan before verification.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-root " PATH"
Inspect or modify the filesystem rooted at absolute
.IR PATH .
The default is the live root
.IR / .
An offline dry run may be performed by a non-root user who can read and search
the required paths.
A live-root operation, every apply, and every rollback require effective UID 0.
.PP
In rollback mode, omission selects the canonical root recorded in the
transaction manifest.
An explicitly supplied root must match the recorded path, device, and inode.
.TP
.BI \-\-output-dir " NEW_PATH"
Create one new dry-run or apply transaction generation at absolute
.IR NEW_PATH .
The path must not exist.
Its physical parent chain must contain no symbolic-link component and must not
be replaceable by an untrusted writer.
The patcher creates the directory with mode 0700 and regular artifacts with
mode 0600.
This option is required for dry-run and manual apply. It is optional with
.B \-\-automatic
and is not used with
.BR \-\-rollback .
.TP
.BI \-\-checks " U-16,U-18"
Select a comma-separated nonempty subset of supported criteria.
The default is U-12,U-16,U-18,U-19,U-22,U-29,U-37.
Values are case-insensitive, whitespace is removed, and duplicates and
unsupported codes are rejected.
This option is not accepted with
.B \-\-automatic
or
.BR \-\-rollback .
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Apply the complete current plan, verify each selected postcondition, and run an
independent post-scan.
This option is required only for manual apply;
.B \-\-automatic
implies the same apply phase.
It cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-rollback .
.TP
.B \-\-automatic
Assess and orchestrate U-01 through U-67 from the required desired-state
profile. This option implies apply and requires effective UID 0 for live and
offline roots. It cannot be combined with
.B \-\-checks
or
.BR \-\-rollback .
.PP
When
.B \-\-output-dir
is omitted, atomically create a unique protected transaction below
.IR /var/lib/kisa-cce-patcher/transactions .
An explicit output directory retains the normal new-path and trusted-parent
requirements.
.TP
.BI \-\-desired-state " FILE"
Read a schema-version-2 YAML profile containing exactly one desired state for
every U-01 through U-67 criterion. This option is required with, and valid only
with,
.BR \-\-automatic .
Fixed rows require input type
.B none
and value
.BR \- .
Conditional rows require the exact registered input type and a nonempty value.
The YAML input path must be absolute. It must identify a readable root-owned,
single-linked, non-symlink regular file with no group or other write bit and a
parent chain that a non-root writer cannot replace.
.TP
.BI \-\-rollback " TRANSACTION_DIR"
Restore a fixed-rule or all-domain transaction from an absolute directory in an
eligible apply or recovery state.
The patcher verifies its schemas, scanner version, immutable-file checksums,
ownership, modes, root identity, fixed rules, target inodes, and current
metadata before changing any target.
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Print command help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the patcher version and exit.
.PP
Options that take values accept both
.B "\-\-option VALUE"
and
.B "\-\-option=VALUE"
forms.
Empty values and positional arguments are rejected.
.SH FIXED RULES
.TP
.B U-12
Create or replace
.I /etc/profile.d/99-kisa-cce-session-timeout.sh
with the fixed 600-second readonly exported TMOUT profile. The configuration
transaction preserves a fingerprinted backup when a file already exists.
.TP
.B U-16
Set the owner UID of
.I /etc/passwd
to 0 and remove permission bits beyond 0644.
Absence is a preflight error.
.TP
.B U-18
Set the owner UID of
.I /etc/shadow
to 0 and remove permission bits beyond 0400.
The patcher does not create a missing file.
.TP
.B U-19
Set the owner UID of
.I /etc/hosts
to 0 and remove permission bits beyond 0644.
Absence is a preflight error.
.TP
.B U-22
For an existing vulnerable
.IR /etc/services ,
set the owner UID to 0 and remove permission bits beyond 0644.
Absence is non-applicable.
The scanner also accepts the
.B bin
and
.B sys
owners; an already conforming file is not added to a transaction.
These names are resolved from the selected root's
.I /etc/passwd
rather than host NSS.
.TP
.B U-29
For an existing vulnerable
.IR /etc/hosts.lpd ,
set the owner UID to 0 and remove permission bits beyond 0600.
Absence is conforming and causes no change.
.TP
.B U-37
Enumerate supported cron and at commands and related configuration and spool
files. Commands become root-owned, no broader than 0750, and lose set-ID bits.
Related files become root-owned and no broader than 0640. Every target has a
separate transaction and rollback record.
.TP
.B U-62
Create or replace
.IR /etc/issue ,
.IR /etc/issue.net ,
and
.I /etc/motd
with the fixed authorization warning. This rule requires an explicit live-root
selection so the post-scan can evaluate active banner surfaces.
.TP
.B U-67
On an explicitly selected live root, enumerate files and directories below
.IR /var/log .
Files become root-owned and no broader than 0644. Directories become root-owned
and lose group and other write permission without gaining any bit. Every target
has a separate transaction and rollback record. Offline U-67 is not actionable
because the patcher cannot establish its log mount boundary.
.PP
Metadata modes are calculated by masking the current mode with the rule maximum,
so those rules cannot grant a bit that was absent. Metadata rules preserve the
existing GID. Managed U-12 and U-62 files use
root:root ownership and mode 0644 or stricter.
.SH REMEDIATION COVERAGE
The installed coverage contract contains 67 unique criterion rows:
9
.BR fixed ,
58
.BR conditional ,
and 0
.BR gated .
A fixed row needs no organization-supplied value. A conditional row has a typed
adapter boundary but still requires its registered input, evidence, provider,
and approval boundaries. Every mutating conditional path requires rollback.
.PP
Ordinary dry-run,
.BR \-\-apply ,
and
.B \-\-checks
dispatch the nine fixed rules listed above; their default selection contains
seven.
.B kisa-cce-patch
with
.B \-\-automatic
and
.B \-\-desired-state
dispatches the full 67-row contract.
.SS Private conditional domains
The installed private transaction modules divide the 58 conditional rows into:
.TP
.I _account-transaction.sh
10 account and group criteria.
.TP
.I _pam-transaction.sh
3 PAM and authselect criteria.
.TP
.I _inventory-transaction.sh
6 evidence-bound inventory criteria.
.TP
.I _filesystem-transaction.sh
8 filesystem criteria.
.TP
.I _service-transaction.sh
9 service-disable criteria.
.TP
.I _network-service-transaction.sh
11 NFS, RPC, mail, and DNS criteria.
.TP
.I _edge-service-transaction.sh
8 OpenSSH, Telnet, firewall, FTP, and SNMP criteria.
.TP
.I _system-transaction.sh
3 package, time, and logging criteria. U-67 delegates to the fixed metadata
engine.
.PP
Conditional adapters can return
.B NOT_APPLICABLE
for an absent provider or reject a complex invocation, incomplete evidence, or
runtime uncertainty before mutation. Operations outside their local boundary
return
.B external_action_required
instead of claiming success. U-64 verifies signed repository and advisory
evidence and creates a package-manager simulation, but never updates a package.
.SS Desired-state version 2
The
.I _desired-state-policy.sh
compiler accepts a restricted YAML schema and emits protected TSV. Fixed rows
require input type
.B none
and value
.BR - .
Each conditional row requires the exact input type registered by the coverage
contract and a nonempty value. Risk, domain, postcondition, validator, and
rollback fields come from the contract rather than YAML.
.PP
Automatic mode invokes this compiler internally. The separate
.BR kisa-cce-policy-compile (8)
command continues to compile scanner policy schema version 1.
.SS Automatic all-domain orchestrator
The automatic command invokes
.I _orchestrator.sh
and the domain bridge. It requires a complete 67-row compiled profile and a
complete 67-result pre-scan JSONL file. It binds those inputs, the canonical
root device/inode, ordered domain requests, and plan digests. Registered domain
callbacks are applied in fixed order and rolled back in reverse order.
.PP
Successful domain verification reaches
.BR awaiting_post_scan ,
not
.BR verified .
Only a fresh JSONL report containing every U-01 through U-67 result exactly
once, with every status equal to
.B GOOD
or
.BR NOT_APPLICABLE ,
can reach
.BR verified .
Any other, missing, duplicate, malformed, or reused result triggers guarded
rollback.
.PP
Complex desired-state values are absolute paths to root-owned, mode-0600 domain
input TSV files. Symbolic links, repeated separators, explicit dot or parent
components, invalid records, and untrusted callback paths are rejected.
.PP
If U-64 or another prerequisite requires an external action, planning stops
before mutation, emits
.BR automatic_status=external_action_required ,
and exits with status 3.
.PP
The orchestrator and domain adapters expose protected transaction loaders and
cross-process rollback. Strict rollback requires the recorded applied state;
transition rollback accepts only bounded interrupted-recovery states. Trusted
orchestrator callbacks are registered functions from loaded private modules.
Where a domain invokes an external callback, its executable must be an absolute
root-owned regular file with a root-owned, non-writable parent chain. These APIs
do not provide a crash-atomic multi-subsystem commit.
.SH TRANSACTION FLOW
.SS Fixed-rule preflight
The command invokes
.BR kisa-cce-scan (8)
in audit and verbose mode for exactly the selected criteria.
A
.B MANUAL
or
.B ERROR
result blocks the operation.
The patcher rejects a scanner and engine state disagreement, an unsafe or
symbolic-link path component, an object type unsupported by its rule, a
hard-linked regular file, inaccessible metadata, and root or target drift.
.PP
A policy result closed to
.B VULNERABLE
because an attestation is absent remains remediation-ineligible and is rejected.
Technical, runtime, or external incomplete evidence is an error rather than a
patch instruction.
.PP
It records the root and target type, device, inode, UID, GID, mode, link count,
size, mtime, and ctime. Regular files also receive a SHA-256 content digest
using a stat-hash-stat stable capture; directories use a no-content sentinel.
Configuration rules additionally bind fixed payloads, original backups, parent
directories, and an apply journal through a configuration manifest.
Every target must pass before mutation begins.
.SS Fixed-rule apply
Apply compares every target with its snapshot before changing it.
It first installs selected fixed configuration payloads, then applies metadata
changes. Metadata operations set the owner UID while retaining the GID and
apply the reduced mode. Both domains verify their expected content or metadata.
Ctime must match before the first mutation and is ignored afterward because the
metadata operations update it.
The complete rollback payload exists before the first change.
.SS Automatic apply
Automatic mode requires a complete desired-state profile, selects U-01 through
U-67, and implies apply. It runs a full audit and creates one protected child
transaction per actionable domain. If no output path was supplied, it creates a
mode-0700 transaction named
.I transaction-YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ.RANDOM
below
.IR /var/lib/kisa-cce-patcher/transactions .
Success emits
.B "automatic_status=verified transaction=PATH"
only after the full post-scan gate, and leaves the transaction in
.B verified
state. Failure emits
.B automatic_status=failed
and returns status 2. A failure after mutation attempts rollback and records
.B rolled_back
or
.B rollback_failed
according to the restoration result.
.PP
An external prerequisite stops before mutation, emits
.B automatic_status=external_action_required
with a bounded prerequisite value, and returns status 3. Complete the external
action and start a new run with fresh evidence.
.SS Post-scan
After an ordinary fixed-rule apply, a new scanner process evaluates the selected
criteria. After automatic domain verification, a new process evaluates all 67.
A vulnerable, manual, erroneous, missing, duplicate, malformed, or reused result
causes apply failure and guarded automatic rollback. Full automatic mode accepts
only a complete report whose 67 statuses are
.B GOOD
or
.BR NOT_APPLICABLE .
Only a successful post-scan reaches transaction state
.BR verified .
.SS Automatic rollback
An apply precondition, mutation, immediate verification, or post-scan failure
after the first change starts reverse-order rollback.
Full automatic rollback visits applied domains and child transactions in
reverse order.
Rollback itself is verified.
If restoration fails, the transaction enters
.B rollback_failed
and requires operator inspection.
.SS Manual rollback
Manual rollback accepts
.BR verified ,
.BR applying ,
.BR applied ,
.BR awaiting_post_scan ,
.BR rollback_in_progress ,
and
.B rollback_failed
states so an interrupted operation remains recoverable.
It rejects
.BR planned ,
.BR external_action_required ,
.BR rolled_back ,
and
.BR failed .
Each target must retain the recorded type, device, inode, size, and mtime.
Regular files must also remain single-linked and match their content digest.
A completed apply must match either the complete post-change metadata or the
already-restored pre-change metadata.
Interrupted recovery permits only combinations of the recorded before and after
UID, GID, and mode values.
Any other drift blocks the complete rollback instead of overwriting a later
administrator or content change.
.PP
Configuration rollback accepts only the exact managed payload or the exact
fingerprinted original content and recorded metadata. It restores U-12 and U-62
configuration before completing metadata rollback.
.PP
The patcher checks every target before the first restoration and repeats the
check during reverse-order rollback.
A concurrent change after preflight can still cause a partial rollback and
.B rollback_failed
state.
Rollback verifies restored metadata but does not run a new scanner process.
.SH TRANSACTION DIRECTORY
A transaction directory can contain:
.TP
.I manifest.tsv
Schema, scanner version, selected criteria, and canonical root identity.
.TP
.I plan.tsv
Every selected rule state, including conforming and non-applicable rules.
.TP
.I metadata.tsv
Immutable before and after UID, GID, and mode, plus device, inode, size, mtime,
ctime, and regular-file SHA-256 content identity for change targets. Directory
rows use a no-content sentinel. Records use schema version 2.
.TP
.I state
Mutable lifecycle state.
.TP
.I checksums.sha256
SHA-256 digests for
.IR manifest.tsv ,
.IR plan.tsv ,
and
.IR metadata.tsv .
When configuration rules are selected, it also covers
.I configuration-plan.tsv
and
.IR configuration/manifest.tsv .
.TP
.I configuration-plan.tsv
Public create-or-replace plan for selected U-12 and U-62 files.
.TP
.I configuration/manifest.tsv
Fingerprinted original state, desired payload, backup identity, and journal
binding for the configuration transaction.
.TP
.I configuration/{backups,payloads,journal}/
Owner-only material transitively bound by the configuration manifest.
.TP
.I pre-scan/
Protected Markdown and JSONL reports from preflight.
.TP
.I post-scan/
Protected Markdown and JSONL reports from post-change verification.
.TP
.I orchestrator/manifest.tsv
Full automatic root identity, input bindings, domain order, request digests, and
plan digests.
.TP
.I orchestrator/manifest.sha256
Integrity digest for the orchestrator manifest.
.TP
.I orchestrator/state
Mutable full automatic lifecycle state.
.TP
.I orchestrator/applied.tsv
Applied-domain recovery order.
.TP
.I orchestrator/inputs/
Protected copies of the compiled desired state and pre-scan JSONL.
.TP
.I orchestrator/domains/
Per-domain requests, plans, applied records, and child transactions.
.PP
The lifecycle values are
.BR planned ,
.BR external_action_required ,
.BR applying ,
.BR applied ,
.BR awaiting_post_scan ,
.BR verified ,
.BR rollback_in_progress ,
.BR rolled_back ,
.BR failed ,
and
.BR rollback_failed .
The state file is excluded from immutable payload checksums and is replaced
atomically during a transition.
.B planned
is the successful dry-run state;
.B verified
is the only successfully applied final state.
Automatic rollback success records
.B rolled_back
even though the failed patch invocation returns status 2.
.SH TERMINAL OUTPUT
Every line uses the scanner dmesg-style prefix with six fractional uptime
digits:
.PP
.EX
[    12.345678] kisa-cce-patch: payload
.EE
.PP
The patcher always writes English progress and does not implement verbose or
debug options.
Automatic lifecycle records use
.BR "automatic_status=started transaction=PATH" ,
.BR "automatic_status=verified transaction=PATH" ,
.BR "automatic_status=external_action_required prerequisite=VALUE" ,
or
.BR automatic_status=failed .
Failure can occur before a transaction path exists.
The started and failed records use standard error; verified and external-action
records and validated transaction paths use standard output. Full apply progress
uses
.B apply_status=started|verified scope=U-01..U-67
and publishes the validated orchestrator manifest path.
Paths and lifecycle states are printed only after the corresponding validation
succeeds.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The dry-run plan, verified apply, or guarded rollback completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Invocation, scanner execution, result parsing, profile, domain, configuration,
or metadata preflight, transaction publication, mutation, verification, or
rollback failed.
.TP
.B 3
A verified external prerequisite is required before mutation.
.TP
.B 130
The process received SIGINT.
.TP
.B 143
The process received SIGTERM.
.PP
An active apply attempts automatic rollback before returning a signal status.
.SH SECURITY
Run the patcher from a trusted package-managed installation and use a protected
local filesystem for transactions.
The command rejects arbitrary rule paths and desired metadata, symbolic links,
changed target inodes, transaction checksum failures, and unrecorded metadata
drift.
.PP
These checks do not provide a filesystem-wide atomic commit.
The implementation does not synchronize every operation to stable storage.
SIGKILL, kernel or storage failure, or power loss can leave a partial apply.
Retain the prewritten transaction for guarded recovery.
.PP
Rollback requires the running scanner version to equal
.I scanner_version
in the manifest.
Complete the rollback window before a package upgrade, or retain a trusted
matching package artifact for recovery.
.PP
The U-12 and U-62 configuration transaction restores its managed content and
recorded owner and mode. Metadata-rule rollback restores UID, GID, and mode
only. Replacement inodes, timestamps, extended attributes, POSIX ACLs,
capabilities, and security labels are outside the transaction record.
A platform can clear set-ID bits or file capabilities during
.BR chown (1),
and
.BR chmod (1)
can affect the POSIX ACL mask; the transaction cannot reconstruct those side
effects.
.PP
Full automatic profiles, domain-input TSV files, pre/post reports, callback
paths, and transaction artifacts are sensitive assessment and change-control
data. Protect them as root-only local files. The command rejects plaintext
secrets and does not interpret a profile or domain input as shell code.
.PP
No package update is executed by the U-64 adapter. Signed evidence and a
package-manager simulation can establish only an external-action prerequisite.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/kisa-cce-linux-scanner/kisa-cce-patcher/
Installed private configuration, metadata, and transaction engine modules.
.TP
.I /var/lib/kisa-cce-patcher/
Recommended protected parent for operator-selected transaction directories.
Automatic mode creates protected
.I transactions/
and transaction children here when no output directory is supplied. The
upstream install target does not create this writable path.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kisa-cce-scan (8)
